  <book-body>
    <book-part book-part-type="chapter">
      <book-part-meta>
        <title-group>
          <title>Foundations</title>
        </title-group>
      </book-part-meta>
      <body>
        <p>The pitch of a note is its fundamental frequency, measured in hertz. Concert A
is <inline-formula><tex-math><![CDATA[440]]></tex-math></inline-formula> Hz, and the perfect fifth above it stands in the ratio <inline-formula><tex-math><![CDATA[3/2]]></tex-math></inline-formula>.</p>
        <statement content-type="definition" id="def:interval">
          <label>Definition 1.1.</label>
          <p>An <italic>interval</italic> is the ratio <inline-formula><tex-math><![CDATA[f_2/f_1]]></tex-math></inline-formula> of the frequencies of two pitches.</p>
        </statement>
        <statement content-type="theorem" id="thm:octave">
          <label>Theorem 1.1.</label>
          <p>Doubling a frequency raises a pitch by exactly one octave.</p>
        </statement>
        <statement content-type="proof">
          <label>Proof.</label>
          <p>An octave is the 2:1 frequency ratio, so doubling a frequency is one octave by definition. ∎</p>
        </statement>
        <p>The equal-tempered scale divides the octave into twelve equal ratios:</p>
        <disp-formula id="eqn:equal">
          <label>(1.1)</label>
          <tex-math><![CDATA[f_n = f_0 \cdot 2^{n/12}]]></tex-math>
        </disp-formula>
        <p>Benson's treatment <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref-Benson2007">Benson2007</xref> develops this from first principles. A
reference to <xref ref-type="statement" rid="thm:octave">theorem 1.1</xref> resolves to the theorem above.<xref ref-type="fn" id="noteref-2" rid="note-2">2</xref></p>
        <fig id="fig:keyboard">
          <label>1.1</label>
          <caption>
            <p>One octave of a piano keyboard.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xlink:href="keyboard.png"/>
        </fig>
      </body>
      <back>
        <fn-group content-type="endnotes">
          <fn id="note-2">
            <label>2</label>
            <p>A foundations footnote, collected at the end of chapter 1.</p>
          </fn>
        </fn-group>
      </back>
    </book-part>
    <book-part book-part-type="chapter">
      <book-part-meta>
        <title-group>
          <title>Consonance and Ratios</title>
        </title-group>
        <contrib-group>
          <contrib contrib-type="author">
            <string-name>Guest Author</string-name>
          </contrib>
        </contrib-group>
      </book-part-meta>
      <body>
        <p>This chapter has its own author — the edited-volume case — distinct from the
book-level author.</p>
        <p>Small whole-number ratios sound consonant. The just-intonation intervals are
collected in <xref ref-type="table" rid="tab:ratios">table 2.1</xref>.</p>
        <table-wrap id="tab:ratios">
          <label>2.1</label>
          <caption>
            <p>Just-intonation interval ratios.</p>
          </caption>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>interval</th>
                <th>ratio</th>
                <th>cents</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>unison</td>
                <td>1/1</td>
                <td>0</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>fifth</td>
                <td>3/2</td>
                <td>702</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>fourth</td>
                <td>4/3</td>
                <td>498</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>octave</td>
                <td>2/1</td>
                <td>1200</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>The fifth and the fourth are the two principal consonances:</p>
        <disp-formula id="eqn:ratios">
          <label>(2.1)</label>
          <tex-math><![CDATA[\begin{aligned}
r_{fifth} &= 3/2 \\
r_{fourth} &= 4/3
\end{aligned}]]></tex-math>
        </disp-formula>
        <p>Sethares <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref-Sethares1993">Sethares1993</xref> ties consonance to timbre rather than to ratios
alone.<xref ref-type="fn" id="noteref-3" rid="note-3">3</xref></p>
      </body>
      <back>
        <fn-group content-type="endnotes">
          <fn id="note-3">
            <label>3</label>
            <p>A consonance footnote, collected at the end of chapter 2.</p>
          </fn>
        </fn-group>
      </back>
    </book-part>
    <book-part book-part-type="chapter">
      <book-part-meta>
        <title-group>
          <title>Visual and Aural Structure</title>
        </title-group>
      </book-part-meta>
      <body>
        <p>Two external notations close the body. A flow of the analysis pipeline:</p>
        <fig id="fig:circle" specific-use="enscribe-dsl-mermaid">
          <label>3.1</label>
          <caption>
            <p>From pitch to perceived interval.</p>
          </caption>
          <alt-text>Mermaid diagram source preserved as preformatted text.</alt-text>
          <preformat preformat-type="mermaid-source">graph LR
  A[Pitch pair] --&gt; B[Frequency ratio]
  B --&gt; C[Cents]
  C --&gt; D[Perceived interval]</preformat>
        </fig>
        <p>And a short tune in ABC notation:</p>
        <fig id="fig:tune" specific-use="enscribe-dsl-abc">
          <label>3.2</label>
          <caption>
            <p>Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star.</p>
          </caption>
          <alt-text>Abc diagram source preserved as preformatted text.</alt-text>
          <preformat preformat-type="abc-source">X:1
T:Twinkle Twinkle
M:4/4
L:1/4
K:C
C C G G | A A G2 | F F E E | D D C2 |</preformat>
        </fig>
        <p>Cross-references <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig:circle">figure 3.1</xref> and <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig:tune">figure 3.2</xref> resolve to the two
figures in this chapter.<xref ref-type="fn" id="noteref-4" rid="note-4">4</xref></p>
      </body>
      <back>
        <fn-group content-type="endnotes">
          <fn id="note-4">
            <label>4</label>
            <p>A structure footnote, collected at the end of chapter 3.</p>
          </fn>
        </fn-group>
      </back>
    </book-part>
